    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Parents want to expose their children to classical music to help them improve concentration, increase spatial intelligence, develop auditory sensibility, and become more cultured. However, parents often struggle to engage their children in classical music, a subject they themselves might not be experts in. Meanwhile, classical music can seem foreign and abstruse for children and adults alike, who have difficulty listening to it because they do not know what to listen for and thus find the music “boring” and “sounding the same.” \r\n\r\nMusicasa is an iPad application that engages children ages 4-8 and their parents in the experience of short, curated selections of classical music to help them develop a deeper and more personal understanding of the music. Designed for families in informal home environments, Musicasa’s games and activities guide children through musical selections and facilitate imaginative conversations between children and parents about the music itself and what the piece sounds like. By encouraging kids and parents to creatively respond to the music via conversation, movement, and art, Musicasa makes classical music more accessible and fun.\r\n\r\nLearners using Musicasa will become more familiar with ways to listen and respond to classical music. In turn, they will cultivate an aural and aesthetic appreciation for the music. Paper and digital prototypes were tested with children ages 3-8 and their parents to assess the application’s content, information, interaction mechanics, and efficacy. Learning was assessed through talk and brief interviews with children and parents around listening to a classical music selection.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
